-- title:
--   Whitehead's lemma for diag(g₁,g₂) over M₂(F)
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a ring and $F$ a field, and let $\varphi : A \simeq M_2(F)$ be a ring isomorphism from $A$ onto the ring of $2 \times 2$ matrices over $F$, where the index type is $\mathrm{Fin}\,2$. Let $g_1, g_2$ be units of $A$ and assume that the determinant of $\varphi(g_1 g_2)$, computed for the product of the images of $g_1$ and $g_2$ in $A$, equals $1$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a finite list $l$ of pairs $(b, t)$ with $b$ a Boolean and $t \in A$ with the following property: send each letter $(b,t)$ to the $2 \times 2$ matrix over $A$ given by $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & t \\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$ when $b$ is true and by $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ t & 1\end{pmatrix}$ when $b$ is false; then the product of the resulting list of matrices, taken in the order of the list, is the block-diagonal matrix $\begin{pmatrix} g_1 & 0 \\ 0 & g_2\end{pmatrix} \in M_2(A)$, the diagonal entries being the images in $A$ of the units $g_1$ and $g_2$. Thus $\mathrm{diag}(g_1,g_2)$ lies in the subgroup of $M_2(A)$ generated by the upper and lower elementary matrices.
--
--   This is the instance of Whitehead's lemma needed here: over a ring isomorphic to $M_2(F)$, a diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(g_1,g_2)$ with $\det \varphi(g_1g_2) = 1$ belongs to the elementary subgroup $E_2(A)$, with the factorisation produced explicitly as a word in upper and lower elementary matrices. It is used in the study of maximal orders in quaternion algebras, in [`QuaternionAlgebra.IsMaximalOrder.exists_matrix_forall_mulVec_mem_iff_mem_ofFiniteIdele_of_forall_finiteAdeleEvalAt_eq_one`](thm.html#QuaternionAlgebra.IsMaximalOrder.exists_matrix_forall_mulVec_mem_iff_mem_ofFiniteIdele_of_forall_finiteAdeleEvalAt_eq_one), to realise an adelic lattice condition by a single matrix built from elementary ones.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Matrix_exists_list_prod_elementary_eq_diagonal_of_det_map_mul_eq_one.lean

